= The Usefulness of Useless Knowledge / 无用知识的用处

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("Is it not a curious fact that in a world steeped in irrational hatreds which threaten civilization itself, men and women—old and young—detach themselves wholly or partly from the angry current of daily life to devote themselves to the cultivation of beauty, to the extension of knowledge, to the cure of disease, to the amelioration of suffering, just as though fanatics were not simultaneously engaged in spreading pain, ugliness, and suffering? The world has always been a sorry and confused sort of place—yet poets and artists and scientists have ignored the factors that would, if attended to, paralyze them.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("这难道不是一件奇妙的事吗？世界浸在非理性的仇恨里，文明本身都受到威胁，男男女女，无论老少，却仍然全部或部分地抽身于日常生活愤怒的洪流，投身于美的培育、知识的拓展、疾病的治疗和痛苦的减轻，仿佛那些狂热者并未同时四处散播痛苦、丑恶与苦难。世界向来是个令人悲哀而困惑的地方；然而，诗人、艺术家和科学家，总能不理会那些一旦顾虑起来便会使自己瘫痪的因素。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("From a practical point of view, intellectual and spiritual life is, on the surface, a useless form of activity, in which men indulge because they procure for themselves greater satisfactions than are otherwise obtainable. In this paper I shall concern myself with the question of the extent to which the pursuit of these useless satisfactions proves unexpectedly the source from which undreamed-of utility is derived.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("从实用的角度看，智识与精神生活，表面上是一种无用的活动。人们投身其中，因为它能带来其他方式无法给予的满足。在本文中，我要讨论的是：追求这些无用的满足，在多大程度上，会出人意料地成为人们从未设想过的实际用途的源泉。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("We hear it said with tiresome iteration that ours is a materialistic age, the main concern of which should be the wider distribution of material goods and worldly opportunities. The justified outcry of those who through no fault of their own are deprived of opportunity and a fair share of worldly goods therefore diverts an increasing number of students from the studies which their fathers pursued to the equally important and no less urgent study of social, economic, and governmental problems.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("我们听到有人反复说，我们是一个物质至上的时代，主要关注的应该是物质商品和世俗机会的更广泛分配。因此，那些并非由于自己的过错而被剥夺了机会和公平分享世俗财富的人的合理抗议，使越来越多的学生从他们的父辈所从事的研究转向同样重要且同样紧迫的社会、经济和政府问题的研究。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("I have no quarrel with this tendency. The world in which we live is the only world about which our senses can testify. Unless it is made a better world, a fairer world, millions will continue to go to their graves silent, saddened, and embittered. I have myself spent many years pleading that our schools should become more acutely aware of the world in which their pupils and students are destined to pass their lives.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("我对这种倾向没有异议。我们生活的世界是我们的感官可以见证的唯一世界。除非世界变得更美好、更公平，否则数百万人将继续沉默、悲伤和痛苦地走向坟墓。我本人多年来一直恳求我们的学校应该更加敏锐地认识到他们的学生和学生注定要在其中度过一生的世界。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("Now I sometimes wonder whether that current has not become too strong and whether there would be sufficient opportunity for a full life if the world were emptied of some of the useless things that give it spiritual significance; in other words, whether our conception of what is useful may not have become too narrow to be adequate to the roaming and capricious possibilities of the human spirit.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("现在我有时会想，这股潮流是否还没有变得太强大，如果世界上清空了一些赋予它精神意义的无用事物，是否还有足够的机会过上充实的生活？换句话说，我们对有用的概念是否已经变得过于狭隘，无法适应人类精神的漫游和反复无常的可能性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("We may look at this question from two points of view: the scientific and the humanistic or spiritual. Let us take the scientific first. I recall a conversation which I had some years ago with Mr. George Eastman on the subject of use. Mr. Eastman, a wise and gentle farseeing man, gifted with taste in music and art, had been saying to me that he meant to devote his vast fortune to the promotion of education in useful subjects.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("我们可以从两个角度来看待这个问题：科学的角度和人文或精神的角度。让我们先从科学角度来说。我记得几年前我与乔治·伊士曼先生就使用问题进行过一次谈话。伊士曼先生是一位睿智、温和、有远见的人，在音乐和艺术方面颇具天赋，他一直对我说，他打算将自己的巨额财富用于促进有用学科的教育。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("I ventured to ask him whom he regarded as the most useful worker in science in the world. He replied instantaneously: “Marconi.” I surprised him by saying, “Whatever pleasure we derive from the radio or however wireless and the radio may have added to human life, Marconi’s share was practically negligible.”")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("我大胆地问他，他认为谁是世界上最有用的科学工作者。他立即回答：“马可尼。”我让他感到惊讶的是，“无论我们从无线电中获得什么乐趣，或者无论无线和无线电给人类生活带来了多大的好处，马可尼的份额几乎可以忽略不计。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("I shall not forget his astonishment on this occasion. He asked me to explain. I replied to him somewhat as follows: “Mr. Eastman, Marconi was inevitable. The real credit for everything that has been done in the field of wireless belongs, as far as such fundamental credit can be definitely assigned to anyone, to Professor Clerk Maxwell, who in 1865 carried out certain abstruse and remote calculations in the field of magnetism and electricity.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("我不会忘记他这次的惊讶。他让我解释一下。我对他的答复大致如下：“伊士曼先生，马可尼是不可避免的。在无线领域所做的一切的真正功劳，就这种基本功劳可以肯定地归于任何人而言，属于克拉克·麦克斯韦教授，他于 1865 年在磁和电领域进行了某些深奥而遥远的计算。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("Maxwell reproduced his abstract equations in a treatise published in 1873. At the next meeting of the British Association Professor H. S. Smith of Oxford declared that ‘no mathematician can turn over the pages of these volumes without realizing that they contain a theory which has already added largely to the methods and resources of pure mathematics.’ Other discoveries supplemented Maxwell's theoretical work during the next fifteen years.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("麦克斯韦在 1873 年发表的一篇论文中重现了他的抽象方程。在英国协会的下一次会议上，牛津大学的 H.S. 史密斯教授宣称，“任何数学家翻阅这些书卷时都不会意识到它们包含的理论已经在很大程度上增加了纯数学的方法和资源。”在接下来的 15 年里，其他发现补充了麦克斯韦的理论工作。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("Finally in 1887 and 1888 the scientific problem still remaining—the detection and demonstration of the electromagnetic waves which are the carriers of wireless signals—was solved by Heinrich Hertz, a worker in Helmholtz’s laboratory in Berlin. Neither Maxwell nor Hertz had any concern about the utility of their work; no such thought ever entered their minds. They had no practical objective.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("最终，在 1887 年和 1888 年，柏林亥姆霍兹实验室的工作人员海因里希·赫兹 (Heinrich Hertz) 解决了仍然存在的科学问题——作为无线信号载体的电磁波的检测和演示。麦克斯韦和赫兹都不关心他们的工作的实用性；他们的脑子里从来没有这样的想法。他们没有实际目标。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("The inventor in the legal sense was of course Marconi, but what did Marconi invent? Merely the last technical detail, mainly the now obsolete receiving device called coherer, almost universally discarded.”")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("法律意义上的发明人当然是马可尼，但是马可尼发明了什么？仅仅是最后一个技术细节，主要是现在已经过时的称为 coherer 的接收设备，几乎被普遍丢弃。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("Hertz and Maxwell could invent nothing, but it was their useless theoretical work which was seized upon by a clever technician and which has created new means for communication, utility, and amusement by which men whose merits are relatively slight have obtained fame and earned millions. Who were the useful men? Not Marconi, but Clerk Maxwell and Heinrich Hertz. Hertz and Maxwell were geniuses without thought of use. Marconi was a clever inventor with no thought but use.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("赫兹和麦克斯韦什么也发明不了，但正是他们无用的理论工作被聪明的技术人员抓住，创造了新的通讯、实用和娱乐手段，使那些功绩相对较小的人获得了名声并赚了数百万美元。谁是有用的人？不是马可尼，而是克拉克·麦克斯韦和海因里希·赫兹。赫兹和麦克斯韦是没有想到有用的天才。马可尼是一位聪明的发明家，除了使用之外没有思想。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("The mention of Hertz's name recalled to Mr. Eastman the Hertzian waves, and I suggested that he might ask the physicists of the University of Rochester precisely what Hertz and Maxwell had done; but one thing I said he could be sure of, namely, that they had done their work without thought of use and that throughout the whole history of science most of the really great discoveries which had ultimately proved to be beneficial to mankind had been made by men and women who were driven not by the desire to be useful but merely the desire to satisfy their curiosity.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("一提到赫兹的名字，伊士曼先生就想起了赫兹波，我建议他可以向罗切斯特大学的物理学家询问赫兹和麦克斯韦到底做了什么；但我说过，他可以肯定一件事，那就是，他们所做的工作并没有考虑有用性，而且在整个科学史上，大多数最终被证明对人类有益的真正伟大的发现都是由男男女女做出的，他们的驱动因素不是有用的欲望，而仅仅是满足好奇心的欲望。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("“Curiosity?” asked Mr. Eastman.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("“好奇心？”伊士曼先生问道。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("“Yes,” I replied, “curiosity, which may or may not eventuate in something useful, is probably the outstanding characteristic of modern thinking. It is not new. It goes back to Galileo, Bacon, and to Sir Isaac Newton, and it must be absolutely unhampered.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("“是的，”我回答道，“好奇心可能是现代思维的突出特征，它可能会或可能不会产生有用的东西。它并不新鲜。它可以追溯到伽利略、培根和艾萨克·牛顿爵士，而且它必须绝对不受阻碍。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("Institutions of learning should be devoted to the cultivation of curiosity and the less they are deflected by considerations of immediacy of application, the more likely they are to contribute not only to human welfare but to the equally important satisfaction of intellectual interest which may indeed be said to have become the ruling passion of intellectual life in modern times.”")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("学习机构应该致力于培养好奇心，它们越少受到应用即时性的影响，它们就越有可能不仅为人类福祉做出贡献，而且为同样重要的知识兴趣的满足做出贡献，这确实可以说已经成为现代知识生活的主导激情。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("What is true of Heinrich Hertz working quietly and unnoticed in a corner of Helmholtz’s laboratory in the later years of the nineteenth century may be said of scientists and mathematicians the world over for several centuries past. We live in a world that would be helpless without electricity. Called upon to mention a discovery of the most immediate and far-reaching practical use we might well agree upon electricity.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("十九世纪后期，海因里希·赫兹（Heinrich Hertz）在亥姆霍兹实验室的一角默默地、不被人注意地工作，这也适用于过去几个世纪以来世界各地的科学家和数学家。我们生活在一个没有电就无助的世界。如果有人要求我们提及一项最直接、影响最深远的实际用途的发现，我们很可能会同意电。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("But who made the fundamental discoveries out of which the entire electrical development of more than one hundred years has come?")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("但一百多年来整个电气发展的基本发现是谁做出的呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("The answer is interesting. Michael Faraday’s father was a blacksmith; Michael himself was apprenticed to a bookbinder. In 1812, when he was already twenty-one years of age, a friend took him to the Royal Institution where he heard Sir Humphrey Davy deliver four lectures on chemical subjects. He kept notes and sent a copy of them to Davy. The very next year, 1813, he became an assistant in Davy's laboratory, working on chemical problems. Two years later he accompanied Davy on a trip to the Continent.")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("答案很有趣。迈克尔·法拉第的父亲是一名铁匠。迈克尔本人是一位装订工的学徒。 1812 年，当他已经二十一岁的时候，一位朋友带他去了皇家学会，在那里他聆听了汉弗莱·戴维爵士 (Sir Humphrey Davy) 发表了四场关于化学主题的讲座。他记下笔记并将其副本发送给戴维。第二年，即 1813 年，他成为戴维实验室的助理，研究化学问题。两年后，他陪同戴维去欧洲大陆旅行。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("In 1825, when he was thirty-four years of age, he became Director of the Laboratory of the Royal Institution where he spent fifty-four years of his life.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("1825年，34岁的他成为皇家研究所的实验室主任，并在那里度过了54年的人生。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("Faraday’s interest soon shifted from chemistry to electricity and magnetism, to which he devoted the rest of his active life. Important but puzzling work in this field had been previously accomplished by Oersted, Ampere, and Wollaston. Faraday cleared away the difficulties which they had left unsolved and by 1841 had succeeded in the task of induction of the electric current.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("法拉第的兴趣很快从化学转向电学和磁学，并把他的余生奉献给了这两个领域。奥斯特、安培和沃拉斯顿此前已经完成了该领域重要但令人费解的工作。法拉第解决了他们尚未解决的困难，并于 1841 年成功地完成了感应电流的任务。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("Four years later a second and equally brilliant epoch in his career opened when he discovered the effect of magnetism on polarized light. His earlier discoveries have led to the infinite number of practical applications by means of which electricity has lightened the burdens and increased the opportunities of modern life. His later discoveries have thus far been less prolific of practical results. What difference did this make to Faraday? Not the least.")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("四年后，当他发现磁力对偏振光的影响时，他的职业生涯开启了第二个同样辉煌的时代。他早期的发现带来了无数的实际应用，电力减轻了现代生活的负担，增加了机会。迄今为止，他后来的发现尚未产生实际成果。这对法拉第有什么影响？并非最不重要的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("At no period of his unmatched career was he interested in utility. He was absorbed in disentangling the riddles of the universe, at first chemical riddles, in later periods, physical riddles. As far as he cared, the question of utility was never raised. Any suspicion of utility would have restricted his restless curiosity. In the end, utility resulted, but it was never a criterion to which his ceaseless experimentation could be subjected.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("在他无与伦比的职业生涯中，他从未对实用性感兴趣。他全神贯注于解开宇宙之谜，起初是化学之谜，后来是物理之谜。就他而言，实用性问题从未被提出。任何对实用性的怀疑都会限制他焦躁不安的好奇心。最终，实用性产生了，但这从来都不是他不断进行实验的标准。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("In the atmosphere which envelopes the world to-day it is perhaps timely to emphasize the fact that the part played by science in making war more destructive and more horrible was an unconscious and unintended by-product of scientific activity. Lord Rayleigh, president of the British Association for the Advancement of Science, in a recent address points out in detail how the folly of man, not the intention of the scientists, is responsible for the destructive use of the agents employed in modern warfare.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("在当今笼罩世界的气氛中，也许应该及时强调这样一个事实：科学在使战争更具破坏性和更加可怕方面所发挥的作用是科学活动的无意识和无意的副产品。英国科学促进会主席雷利勋爵在最近的一次演讲中详细指出，现代战争中使用的药剂的破坏性使用是由于人类的愚蠢而不是科学家的意图造成的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("The innocent study of the chemistry of carbon compounds, which has led to infinite beneficial results, showed that the action of nitric acid on substances like benzene, glycerine, cellulose, etc., resulted not only in the beneficent aniline dye industry but in the creation of nitroglycerine, which has uses good and bad.")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("对碳化合物化学的天真研究带来了无限有益的结果，表明硝酸与苯、甘油、纤维素等物质的作用，不仅导致了有益的苯胺染料工业，而且导致了硝化甘油的产生，硝化甘油的用途有好有坏。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("Somewhat later Alfred Nobel, turning to the same subject, showed that by mixing nitro-glycerine with other substances, solid explosives which could be safely handled could be produced-among others, dynamite. It is to dynamite that we owe our progress in mining, in the making of such railroad tunnels as those which now pierce the Alps and other mountain ranges; but of course dynamite has been abused by politicians and soldiers.")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("稍后，阿尔弗雷德·诺贝尔转向同一主题，表明通过将硝基甘油与其他物质混合，可以生产可以安全处理的固体炸药，其中包括炸药。正是由于炸药，我们才在采矿业、修建铁路隧道（例如现在穿越阿尔卑斯山和其他山脉的铁路隧道）方面取得了进步。但当然，炸药已被政客和士兵滥用。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("Scientists are, however, no more to blame than they are to blame for an earthquake or a flood. The same thing can be said of poison gas. Pliny was killed by breathing sulphur dioxide in the eruption of Vesuvius almost two thousand years ago. Chlorine was not isolated by scientists for warlike purposes, and the same is true of mustard gas.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("然而，科学家的责任并不比地震或洪水的责任更大。毒气也是如此。大约两千多年前，普林尼在维苏威火山喷发时因吸入二氧化硫而丧生。科学家分离氯并不是为了战争目的，芥子气也是如此。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("These substances could be limited to beneficent use, but when the airplane was perfected, men whose hearts were poisoned and whose brains were addled perceived that the airplane, an innocent invention, the result of long disinterested and scientific effort, could be made an instrument of destruction, of which no one had ever dreamed and at which no one had ever deliberately aimed.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("这些物质可以仅限于有益的用途，但是当飞机被完善时，那些心灵被毒害、大脑被迷惑的人们意识到，飞机这个无辜的发明，是长期无私的科学努力的结果，可能会成为一种毁灭性的工具，没有人梦想过，也没有人故意瞄准它。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("In the domain of higher mathematics almost innumerable instances can be cited. For example, the most abstruse mathematical work of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries was the “NonEuclidian Geometry.” Its inventor, Gauss, though recognized by his contemporaries as a distinguished mathematician, did not dare to publish his work on “Non-Euclidian Geometry” for a quarter of a century.")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("在高等数学领域，几乎可以引用无数的例子。例如，十八、十九世纪最深奥的数学著作是《非欧几何》。它的发明者高斯虽然被同时代人公认为一位杰出的数学家，但在长达四分之一个世纪的时间里却不敢发表他关于“非欧几里得几何”的著作。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("As a matter of fact, the theory of relativity itself with all its infinite practical bearings would have been utterly impossible without the work which Gauss did at Gottingen.")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("事实上，如果没有高斯在哥廷根所做的工作，相对论本身及其无限的实用意义是完全不可能出现的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("Again, what is known now as “group theory” was an abstract and inapplicable mathematical theory. It was developed by men who were curious and whose curiosity and puttering led them into strange paths; but “group theory” is to-day the basis of the quantum theory of spectroscopy, which is in daily use by people who have no idea as to how it came about.")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("同样，现在所谓的“群论”是一种抽象且不适用的数学理论。它是由那些充满好奇心的人开发出来的，他们的好奇心和犹豫把他们引向了奇怪的道路。但“群论”如今是光谱学量子理论的基础，人们每天都在使用它，但他们不知道它是如何产生的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("The whole calculus of probability was discovered by mathematicians whose real interest was the rationalization of gambling. It has failed of the practical purpose at which they aimed, but it has furnished a scientific basis for all types of insurance, and vast stretches of nineteenth century physics are based upon it. From a recent number of Science I quote the following:")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("整个概率计算是由数学家发现的，他们真正的兴趣是赌博的合理化。它未能达到他们所期望的实际目的，但它为所有类型的保险提供了科学基础，并且十九世纪物理学的大部分内容都是以它为基础的。我从最近的一些《科学》杂志中引用了以下内容：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("The stature of Professor Albert Einstein's genius reached new heights when it was disclosed that the learned mathematical physicist developed mathematics fifteen years ago which are now helping to solve the mysteries of the amazing fluidity of helium near the absolute zero of the temperature scale. Before the symposium on intermolecular action of the American Chemical Society Professor F. London, of the University of Paris, now visiting professor at Duke University, credited Professor Einstein with the concept of an \"ideal\" gas which appeared in papers published in 1924 and 1925.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("当阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦教授的天才地位达到新的高度时，这位博学的数学物理学家在十五年前发展了数学，现在正在帮助解决氦在温标绝对零附近的惊人流动性的奥秘。在美国化学会分子间作用研讨会之前，巴黎大学的 F. London 教授（现杜克大学客座教授）将“理想”气体的概念归功于爱因斯坦教授，该概念出现在 1924 年和 1925 年发表的论文中。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S034")[#text("The Einstein 1925 reports were not about relativity theory, but discussed problems seemingly without any practical significance at the time. They described the degeneracy of an “ideal” gas near the lower limits of the scale of temperature. Because all gases were known to be condensed to liquids at the temperatures in question, scientists rather overlooked the Einstein work of fifteen years ago.")]

#zh(id: "S034")[#text("爱因斯坦1925年的报告并不是关于相对论的，而是讨论了当时看似没有任何实际意义的问题。他们描述了“理想”气体在温度下限附近的简并性。由于已知所有气体在所讨论的温度下都会凝结成液体，因此科学家们相当忽视了爱因斯坦十五年前的工作。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S035")[#text("However, the recently discovered behavior of liquid helium has brought the side-tracked Einstein concept to new usefulness. Most liquids increase in viscosity, become stickier and flow less easily, when they become colder. The phrase “colder than molasses in January” is the layman's concept of viscosity and a correct one.")]

#zh(id: "S035")[#text("然而，最近发现的液氦行为使偏离轨道的爱因斯坦概念有了新的用途。当温度变冷时，大多数液体的粘度都会增加，变得更粘并且不易流动。 “比一月糖蜜还冷”这句话是外行人对粘度的概念，也是正确的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S036")[#text("Liquid helium, however, is a baffling exception. At the temperature known as the “delta” point, only 2.19 degrees above absolute zero, liquid helium flows better than it does at higher temperatures and, as a matter of fact, the liquid helium is about as nebulous as a gas. Added puzzles in its strange behavior include its enormous ability to conduct heat. At the delta point it is about 500 times as effective in this respect as copper at room temperature.")]

#zh(id: "S036")[#text("然而，液氦是一个令人困惑的例外。在被称为“δ”点的温度下，仅比绝对零度高 2.19 度，液氦比在更高温度下流动得更好，事实上，液氦与气体一样模糊。其奇怪的行为增加了一些谜团，包括其巨大的导热能力。在 Delta 点，它在这方面的效果大约是室温下铜的 500 倍。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S037")[#text("Liquid helium, with these and other anomalies, has posed a major mystery for physicists and chemists.")]

#zh(id: "S037")[#text("液氦的这些和其他异常现象给物理学家和化学家带来了一个重大谜团。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S038")[#text("Professor London stated that the interpretation of the behavior of liquid helium can best be explained by considering it as a Bose-Einstein “ideal” gas, by using the mathematics worked out in 1924–25, and by taking over also some of the concepts of the electrical conduction of metals. By simple analogy, the amazing fluidity of liquid helium can be partially explained by picturing the fluidity as something akin to the wandering of electrons in metals to explain electrical conduction.")]

#zh(id: "S038")[#text("London 教授表示，对液氦行为的最好解释是，将其视为玻色-爱因斯坦“理想”气体，使用 1924-25 年制定的数学模型，并采用金属导电的一些概念。通过简单的类比，液氦惊人的流动性可以通过将流动性想象成类似于金属中电子的漫游来解释导电性来部分解释。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S039")[#text("Let us look in another direction. In the domain of medicine and public health the science of bacteriology has played for half a century the leading role. What is its story? Following the Franco-Prossian War of 1870, the German Government founded the great University of Strasbourg. Its first professor of anatomy was Wilhelm von Waldeyer, subsequently professor of anatomy in Berlin.")]

#zh(id: "S039")[#text("让我们看看另一个方向。在医学和公共卫生领域，细菌学半个世纪以来一直发挥着主导作用。它的故事是什么？ 1870 年法普战争后，德国政府建立了伟大的斯特拉斯堡大学。它的第一位解剖学教授是威廉·冯·瓦尔德耶（Wilhelm von Waldeyer），后来在柏林担任解剖学教授。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S040")[#text("In his Reminiscences he relates that among the students who went with him to Strasbourg during his first semester there was a small, inconspicuous, self-contained youngster of seventeen by name Paul Ehrlich. The usual course in anatomy then consisted of dissection and microscopic examination of tissues. Ehrlich paid little or no attention to dissection, but, as Waldeyer remarks in his Remimscences:")]

#zh(id: "S040")[#text("在他的《回忆录》中，他提到，在第一学期和他一起去斯特拉斯堡的学生中，有一个身材矮小、不起眼、自给自足的十七岁年轻人，名叫保罗·埃利希(Paul Ehrlich)。解剖学的通常课程包括组织的解剖和显微镜检查。埃利希很少或根本不关注解剖，但是，正如瓦尔德耶在他的回忆录中所说：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S041")[#text("I noticed quite early that Ehrlich would work long hours at his desk, completely absorbed in microscopic observation. Moreover, his desk gradually became covered with colored spots of every description. As I saw him sitting at work one day, I went up to him and asked what he was doing with all hisrainbow array of colors on his table.")]

#zh(id: "S041")[#text("我很早就注意到埃利希会在办公桌前长时间工作，完全专注于微观观察。而且，他的办公桌上也渐渐布满了各种各样的彩色斑点。有一天，当我看到他坐在工作岗位上时，我走到他面前，问他桌子上的彩虹色阵列在做什么。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S042")[#text("Thereupon this young student in his first semester supposedly pursuing the regular course in anatomy looked up at me and blandly remarked, “Ich probiere.” This might be freely translated, “I am trying” or “I am just fooling.” I replied to him, “Very well. Go on with your fooling.” Soon I saw that without any teaching or direction whatsoever on my part I possessed in Ehrlich a student of unusual quality.")]

#zh(id: "S042")[#text("于是，这个在第一学期修读解剖学常规课程的年轻学生抬头看着我，温和地说：“我是 Probiere。”这可以自由地翻译为“我正在尝试”或“我只是在开玩笑”。我回答他：“好吧，你继续胡闹吧。”很快我发现，在没有任何我的教导或指导的情况下，我在埃利希身上拥有了一位品质非凡的学生。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S043")[#text("Waldeyer wisely left him alone. Ehrlich made his way precariously through the medical curriculum and ultimately procured his degree mainly because it was obvious to his teachers that he had no intention of ever putting his medical degree to practical use. He went subsequently to Breslau where he worked under Professor Cohnheim, the teacher of our own Dr. Welch, founder and maker of the Johns Hopkins Medical School. I do not suppose that the idea of use ever crossed Ehrlich's mind. He was interested.")]

#zh(id: "S043")[#text("瓦尔德尔明智地让他一个人呆着。埃利希在医学课程上的学习之路岌岌可危，并最终获得了学位，主要是因为他的老师很明显他无意将他的医学学位付诸实践。随后，他前往布雷斯劳，在科恩海姆教授的指导下工作，科恩海姆教授是约翰·霍普金斯医学院的创始人和创始人韦尔奇博士的老师。我认为埃利希从来没有想到过使用的想法。他很感兴趣。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S044")[#text("He was curious; he kept on fooling. Of course his fooling was guided by a deep instinct, but it was a purely scientific, not an utilitarianmotivation. What resulted? Koch and his associates established a new science, the science of bacteriology. Ehrlich's experiments were now applied by a fellow student, Weigert, to staining bacteria and thereby assisting in their differentiation.")]

#zh(id: "S044")[#text("他很好奇；他继续愚弄。当然，他的愚弄是出于一种深刻的本能，但这是纯粹的科学动机，而不是功利主义动机。结果是什么？科赫和他的同事建立了一门新的科学，即细菌学。埃利希的实验现在被他的同学魏格特应用到对细菌进行染色，从而帮助它们分化。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S045")[#text("Ehrlich himself developed the staining of the blood film with the dyes on which our modern knowledge of the morphology of the blood corpuscles, red and white, is based. Not a day passes but that in thousands of hospitals the world over Ehrlich's technic is employed in the examination of the blood. Thus the apparently aimless fooling in Waldeyer's dissecting room in Strasbourg has become a main factor in the daily practice of medicine.")]

#zh(id: "S045")[#text("埃利希本人发明了用染料对血膜进行染色，这是我们对血球（红色和白色）形态的现代知识的基础。全世界数以千计的医院每天都采用艾利希技术进行血液检查。因此，斯特拉斯堡瓦尔德耶解剖室中看似漫无目的的愚弄已成为日常医学实践中的一个主要因素。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S046")[#text("I shall give one example from industry, one selected at random; for there are scores besides. Professor Berl, of the Carnegie Institute of Technology (Pittsburgh) writes as follows:")]

#zh(id: "S046")[#text("我将举一个来自工业界的例子，一个随机选择的例子；因为除此之外还有分数。卡内基理工学院（匹兹堡）的贝尔教授写道：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S047")[#text("The founder of the modern rayon industry was the French Count Chardonnet. It is known that he used a solution of nitro cotton in ether-alcohol, and that he pressed this viscous solution through capillaries into water which served to coagulate the cellulose nitrate filament. After the coagulation, this filament entered the air and was wound up on bobbins. One day Chardonnet inspected his French factory at Besan••on. By an accident the water which should coagulate the cellulose nitrate filament was stopped.")]

#zh(id: "S047")[#text("现代人造丝工业的创始人是法国夏多内伯爵。众所周知，他使用了硝基棉的乙醚醇溶液，并将这种粘稠溶液通过毛细管压入水中，从而凝固了硝酸纤维素长丝。凝固后，该细丝进入空气并卷绕在线轴上。有一天，夏多内视察了他位于贝桑翁的法国工厂。由于意外，本应凝固硝酸纤维素长丝的水被停止。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S048")[#text("The workmen found that the spinning operation went much better without water than with water. This was the birthday of the very important process of dry spinning, which is actually carried out on the greatest scale.")]

#zh(id: "S048")[#text("工人们发现，在没有水的情况下，纺纱作业比有水的情况要好得多。这是干纺这个非常重要的工艺的诞生日，该工艺实际上是在最大规模的情况下进行的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S049")[#text("I am not for a moment suggesting that everything that goes on in laboratories will ultimately turn to some unexpected practical use or that an ultimate practical use is its actual justification. Much more am I pleading for the abolition of the word \"use,\" and for the freeing of the human spirit. To be sure, we shall thus free some harmless cranks. To be sure, we shall thus waste some precious dollars.")]

#zh(id: "S049")[#text("我暂时无意暗示实验室中发生的一切最终都会转化为某种意想不到的实际用途，或者最终的实际用途才是其真正的合理性。我更呼吁废除“使用”这个词，并解放人类精神。可以肯定的是，我们将因此释放一些无害的怪人。可以肯定的是，我们将因此浪费一些宝贵的美元。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S050")[#text("But what is infinitely more important is that we shall be striking the shackles off the human mind and setting it free for the adventures which in our own day have, on the one hand, taken Hale and Rutherford and Einstein and their peers millions upon millions of miles into the uttermost realms of space and, on the other, loosed the boundless energy imprisoned in the atom.")]

#zh(id: "S050")[#text("但更重要的是，我们将摆脱人类思想的束缚，让它自由地进行冒险，在我们这个时代，这些冒险一方面将黑尔、卢瑟福、爱因斯坦和他们的同辈带到了数百万英里的太空领域，另一方面释放了囚禁在原子中的无限能量。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S051")[#text("What Rutherford and others like Bohr and Millikan have done out of sheer curiosity in the effort to understand the construction of the atom has released forces which may transform human life; but this ultimate and unforeseen and unpredictable practical result is not offered as a justification for Rutherford or Einstein or Millikan or Bohr or any of their peers. Let them alone. No educational administrator can possibly direct the channels in which these or other men shall work.")]

#zh(id: "S051")[#text("卢瑟福和玻尔、密立根等人出于纯粹的好奇心，努力了解原子的结构，所做的一切释放了可能改变人类生活的力量；但这种最终的、不可预见的和不可预测的实际结果并没有为卢瑟福、爱因斯坦、密立根、玻尔或他们的任何同行提供理由。让他们去吧。任何教育管理者都不可能指导这些人或其他人的工作渠道。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S052")[#text("The waste, I admit again, looks prodigious. It is not really so. All the waste that could be summed up in developing the science of bacteriology is as nothing compared to the advantages which have accrued from the discoveries of Pasteur, Koch, Ehrlich, Theobald Smith, and scores of others-advantages that could never have accrued if the idea of possible use had permeated their . minds.")]

#zh(id: "S052")[#text("我再次承认，这种浪费看起来是巨大的。事实并非如此。与巴斯德、科赫、埃利希、西奥博尔德·史密斯和其他许多人的发现所带来的优势相比，发展细菌学所造成的所有浪费都是微不足道的——如果可能使用的想法渗透到了他们的思想中，这些优势就永远不会产生。头脑。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S053")[#text("These great artists-for such are scientists and bacteriologists-disseminated the spirit which prevailed in laboratories in which they were simply following the line of their own natural curiosity.")]

#zh(id: "S053")[#text("这些伟大的艺术家——例如科学家和细菌学家——传播了实验室里流行的精神，在实验室里他们只是遵循自己天生的好奇心。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S054")[#text("I am not criticising institutions like schools of engineering or law in which the usefulness motive necessarily predominates. Not infrequently the tables are turned, and practical difficulties encountered in industry or in laboratories stimulate theoretical inquiries which may or may not solve the problems by which they were suggested, but may also open up new vistas, uselessat the moment, but pregnant with future achievements, practical and theoretical.")]

#zh(id: "S054")[#text("我并不是在批评像工程学院或法学院这样的机构，因为在这些机构中，功用动机必然占主导地位。情况经常会发生逆转，工业界或实验室中遇到的实际困难会激发理论探究，这些理论探究可能会也可能不会解决所提出的问题，但也可能开辟新的前景，虽然目前无用，但孕育着未来的实践和理论成就。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S055")[#text("With the rapid accumulation of \"useless\" or theoretic knowledge a situation has been created in which it has become increasingly possible to attack practical problems in a scientific spirit. Not only inventors, but \"pure\" scientists have indulged in this sport. I have mentioned Marconi, an inventor, who, while a benefactor to the human race, as a matter of fact merely \"picked other men's brains.\" Edison belongs to the same category. Pasteur was different.")]

#zh(id: "S055")[#text("随着“无用的”知识或理论知识的迅速积累，已经创造了一种情况，在这种情况下，以科学精神解决实际问题变得越来越可能。不仅是发明家，就连“纯粹”的科学家也沉迷于这项运动。我曾提到过马可尼，一位发明家，他虽然是人类的恩人，但实际上只是“采摘了别人的大脑”。爱迪生属于同一类别。巴斯德则不同。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S056")[#text("He was a great scientist; but he was not averse to attacking practical problems-such as the condition of French grapevines or the problems of beer-brewing-and not only solving the immediate difficulty, but also wresting from the practical problem some far-reaching theoretic conclusion, \"useless\" at the moment, but likely in some unforeseen manner to be \"useful\" later.")]

#zh(id: "S056")[#text("他是一位伟大的科学家；但他并不反对解决实际问题——例如法国葡萄藤的状况或啤酒酿造问题——不仅解决眼前的困难，而且从实际问题中提取一些影响深远的理论结论，这些结论目前“无用”，但很可能以某种不可预见的方式在以后“有用”。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S057")[#text("Ehrlich, fundamentally speculative in his curiosity, turned fiercely upon the problem of syphilis and doggedly pursued it until a solution of immediate practical use-the discovery of salvarsan -was found.")]

#zh(id: "S057")[#text("埃利希出于好奇心，从根本上进行了推测，他猛烈地研究梅毒问题，并顽强地研究它，直到找到了一种可立即实际使用的解决方案——萨尔瓦散的发现。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S058")[#text("The discoveries of insulin by Banting for use in diabetes and of liver extract by Minot and Whipple for use in pernicious anemia belong in the same category: both were made by thoroughly scientific men, who realized that much \"useless\" knowledge had been piled up by men unconcerned with its practical bearings, but that the time was now ripe to raise practical questions in a scientific manner.")]

#zh(id: "S058")[#text("班廷发现的用于治疗糖尿病的胰岛素和迈诺特和惠普尔发现的用于治疗恶性贫血的肝脏提取物属于同一类别：两者都是由彻底的科学人士做出的，他们意识到许多“无用”的知识是由不关心其实际意义的人堆积起来的，但现在以科学的方式提出实际问题的时机已经成熟。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S059")[#text("Thus it becomes obvious that one must be wary in attributing scientific discovery wholly to anyone person. Almost every discovery has a long and precarious history. Someone finds a bit here, another a bit there. A third step succeeds later and thus onward till a genius pieces the bits together and makes the decisive contribution. Science, like the Mississippi, begins in a tiny rivulet in the distant forest. Gradually other streams swell its volume.")]

#zh(id: "S059")[#text("因此，很明显，人们必须谨慎地将科学发现完全归因于任何人。几乎每一项发现都有一段漫长而不稳定的历史。有人在这里找到一点，那里又找到一点。第三步稍后会成功，并继续前进，直到一位天才将这些碎片拼凑起来并做出决定性的贡献。科学就像密西西比河一样，始于遥远森林中的一条小溪。渐渐地，其他溪流的体积也增大了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S060")[#text("And the roaring river that bursts the dikes is formed from countless sources.")]

#zh(id: "S060")[#text("而冲破堤坝的咆哮河流是由无数的源头形成的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S061")[#text("I cannot deal with this aspect exhaustively, but I may in passing say this: over a period of one or two hundred years the contributions of professional schools to their respective activities will probably be found to lie, not so much in the training of men who may to-morrow become practical engineers or practical lawyers or practical doctors, but rather in the fact that even in the pursuit of strictly practical aims an enormous amount of apparently useless activity goes on.")]

#zh(id: "S061")[#text("我无法详尽地讨论这个问题，但我可以顺便说一句：在一两百年的时间里，职业学校对其各自活动的贡献可能会被发现是谎言，与其说是在培养明天可能成为实用工程师、实用律师或实用医生的人，不如说是在事实上，即使在追求严格的实用目标的过程中，大量显然无用的活动仍在继续。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S062")[#text("Out of this useless activity there come discoveries which may well prove of infinitely more importance to the human mind and to the human spirit than the accomplishment of the useful ends for which the schools were founded. The considerations upon which I have touched emphasize-if emphasis were needed-the overwhelming importance of spiritual and intellectual freedom.")]

#zh(id: "S062")[#text("从这种无用的活动中产生的发现很可能证明对人类思想和人类精神比实现学校成立的有用目的更重要。我所涉及的考虑强调——如果需要强调的话——精神和智力自由的压倒性重要性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S063")[#text("I have spoken of experimental science; I have spoken of mathematics; but what I say is equally true of music and art and of every other expression of the untrammeled human spirit. The mere fact that they bring satisfaction to an individual soul bent upon its own purification and elevation is all the justification that they need. And in justifying these without any reference whatsoever, implied or actual, to usefulness we justify colleges, universities, and institutes of research.")]

#zh(id: "S063")[#text("我已经谈到了实验科学；我已经谈到了数学；但我所说的同样适用于音乐和艺术以及不受约束的人类精神的所有其他表达。他们为一心净化和提升的个人灵魂带来满足，这一事实本身就是他们所需要的全部理由。在没有任何暗示或实际参考的情况下证明这些的合理性时，我们证明了学院、大学和研究机构的合理性。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S064")[#text("An institution which sets free successive generations of human souls is amply justified whether or not this graduate or that makes a so-called useful contribution to human knowledge. A poem, a symphony, a painting, a mathematical truth, a new scientific fact, all bear in themselves all the justification that universities, colleges, and institutes of research need or require.")]

#zh(id: "S064")[#text("一个解放一代代人类灵魂的机构是完全合理的，无论这个毕业生或那个人是否对人类知识做出了所谓的有用贡献。一首诗、一首交响乐、一幅画、一个数学真理、一个新的科学事实，它们本身都具有大学、学院和研究机构需要或要求的所有理由。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S065")[#text("The subject which I am discussing has at this moment a peculiar poignancy. In certain large areas-Germany and Italy especially-the effort is now being made to clamp down the freedom of the human spirit. Universities have been so reorganized that they have become tools of those who believe in a special political, economic, or racial creed.")]

#zh(id: "S065")[#text("我现在正在讨论的主题有一种特殊的辛酸。在某些大的地区——尤其是德国和意大利——现在正在努力压制人类精神的自由。大学经过如此重组，以至于它们已成为那些相信特殊政治、经济或种族信条的人的工具。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S066")[#text("Now and then a thoughtless individual in one of the few democracies left in this world will even question the fundamental importance of absolutely untrammeled academic freedom. The real enemy of the human race is not the fearless and irresponsible thinker, be he right or wrong. The real enemy is the man who tries to mold the human spirit so that it will not dare to spread its wings, as its wings were once spread in Italy and Germany, as well as in Great Britain and the United States.")]

#zh(id: "S066")[#text("在这个世界上为数不多的民主国家之一，有时，一个没有思想的人甚至会质疑绝对不受约束的学术自由的根本重要性。人类真正的敌人不是无所畏惧、不负责任的思想家，无论他是对还是错。真正的敌人是那些试图塑造人类精神，使其不敢展开翅膀的人，就像它的翅膀曾经在意大利和德国以及英国和美国展开一样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S067")[#text("This is not a new idea. It was the idea which animated von Humboldt when, in the hour of Germany's conquest by Napoleon, he conceived and founded the University of Berlin. It is the idea which animated President Gilman in the founding of the Johns Hopkins University, after which every university in this country has sought in greater or less degree to remake itself. It is the idea to which every individual who values his immortal soul will be true whatever the personal consequences to himself.")]

#zh(id: "S067")[#text("这并不是一个新想法。在德国被拿破仑征服的时期，正是这个想法激发了冯·洪堡的灵感，他构想并创立了柏林大学。正是这个想法激励了吉尔曼总统创建了约翰·霍普金斯大学，此后这个国家的每一所大学都或多或少地寻求自我改造。每个珍视自己不朽灵魂的人都将忠实于这一理念，无论对自己造成什么个人后果。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S068")[#text("Justification of spiritual freedom goes, however, much farther than originality whether in the realm of science or humanism, for it implies tolerance throughout the range of human dissimilarities.")]

#zh(id: "S068")[#text("然而，无论是在科学领域还是在人文主义领域，精神自由的辩护都比原创性更进一步，因为它意味着对人类差异范围的宽容。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S069")[#text("In the face of the history of the human race what can be more silly or ridiculous than likes or dislikes founded upon race or religion? Does humanity want symphonies and paintings and profound scientific truth, or does it want Christian symphonies, Christian paintings, Christian science, or Jewish symphonies, Jewish paintings, Jewish science, or Mohammedan or Egyptian or J apanese or Chinese or American or German or Russian or Communist or Conservative contributions to and expressions of the infinite richness of the human soul?")]

#zh(id: "S069")[#text("面对人类历史，还有什么比基于种族或宗教的好恶更愚蠢、更可笑的呢？人类想要交响乐、绘画和深刻的科学真理，还是想要基督教交响乐、基督教绘画、基督教科学或犹太交响乐、犹太绘画、犹太科学，或伊斯兰教、埃及人、日本人、中国人、美国人、德国人、俄罗斯人、共产主义或保守派对人类灵魂的无限丰富性的贡献和表达？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S070")[#text("Among the most striking and immediate consequences of foreign intolerance I may, I think, fairly cite the rapid development of the Institute for Advanced Study, established by Mr. Louis Bamberger and his sister, Mrs. Felix Fuld, at Princeton, New Jersey. The founding of the Institute was suggested in 1930.")]

#zh(id: "S070")[#text("我认为，在外国不容忍的最显着和最直接的后果中，我可以合理地引用路易斯·班伯格先生和他的妹妹菲利克斯·富尔德夫人在新泽西州普林斯顿建立的高级研究所的快速发展。 1930年有人提议成立该研究所。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S071")[#text("It was located at Princeton partly because of the founders' attachment to the State of New Jersey, but, in so far as my judgment was concerned, because Princeton had a small graduate school of high quality with which the most intimate cooperation was feasible. To Princeton University the Institute owes a debt that can never be fully appreciated. The work of the Institute with a considerable portion of its personnel began in 1933.")]

#zh(id: "S071")[#text("它位于普林斯顿，部分原因是创始人对新泽西州的依恋，但就我的判断而言，因为普林斯顿有一所高质量的小型研究生院，与之进行最密切的合作是可行的。该研究所欠普林斯顿大学一笔永远无法完全理解的债务。该研究所及其相当一部分人员的工作始于 1933 年。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S072")[#text("On its faculty are eminent American scholars-Veblen, Alexander, and Morse, among the mathematicians; Meritt, Lowe, and Miss Goldman among the humanists; Stewart, Riefler,Warren, Earle, and Mitrany among the publicists and economists. And to these should be added scholars and scientists of equal caliber already assembled in Princeton University, Princeton's library, and its laboratories.")]

#zh(id: "S072")[#text("其教职人员中有著名的美国学者——数学家中的凡勃伦、亚历山大和莫尔斯；梅里特（Meritt）、洛（Lowe）和戈德曼小姐（Miss Goldman）属于人文主义者；政治家和经济学家包括斯图尔特、里夫勒、沃伦、厄尔和米特兰尼。除此之外，还应该包括普林斯顿大学、普林斯顿图书馆及其实验室中已经聚集的同等水平的学者和科学家。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S073")[#text("But the Institute for Advanced Study is indebted to Hitler for Einstein, Weyl, and von Neumann in mathematics; for Herzfeld and Panofsky in the field of humanistic studies, and for a host of younger men who during the past six years have come under the influence of this distinguished group and are already adding to the strength of American scholarship in every section of the land.")]

#zh(id: "S073")[#text("但高等研究院在数学方面要感谢希特勒的爱因斯坦、韦尔和冯·诺依曼；对于人文研究领域的赫茨菲尔德和潘诺夫斯基，以及在过去六年中受到这个杰出团体影响的许多年轻人来说，他们已经在这片土地上为美国学术界的力量增添了力量。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S074")[#text("The Institute is, from the standpoint of organization, the simplest and least formal thing imaginable. It consists of three schools-a School of Mathematics, a School of Humanistic Studies, a School of Economics and Politics. Each school is made up of a permanent group of professors and an annually changing group of members. Each school manages its own affairs as it pleases; within each group each individual disposes of his time and energy as he pleases.")]

#zh(id: "S074")[#text("从组织的角度来看，该研究所是可以想象到的最简单、最不正式的机构。下设数学学院、人文学院、经济政治学院三个学院。每所学校都由一组固定的教授和每年更换的成员组成。各校自行办事；在每个小组中，每个人都可以随心所欲地支配自己的时间和精力。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S075")[#text("The members who already have come from twenty-two foreign countries and thirtynine institutions of higher learning in the United States are admitted, if deemed worthy, by the several groups. They enjoy precisely the same freedom as the professors. They may work with this or that professor, as they severally arrange; they may work alone, consulting from time to time anyone likely to be helpful. No routine isfollowed; no lines are drawn between professors, members, or visitors.")]

#zh(id: "S075")[#text("已经来自 22 个外国国家和 39 所美国高等教育机构的成员，如果认为值得的话，将被几个团体录取。他们享有与教授完全相同的自由。他们可以根据自己的安排与这位或那位教授一起工作；他们可能单独工作，不时向任何可能有帮助的人咨询。不遵循任何常规；教授、会员或访客之间没有界限。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S076")[#text("Princeton students and professors and Institute members and professors mingle so freely as to be indistinguishable. Learning as such is cultivated. The results to the individual and to society are left to take care of themselves. No faculty meetings are held; no committees exist. Thus men with ideas enjoy conditions favorable to reflection and to conference. A mathematician may cultivate mathematics without distraction; so may a humanist in his field, an economist or a student of politics in his.")]

#zh(id: "S076")[#text("普林斯顿大学的学生和教授以及研究所成员和教授自由地交往，难以区分。学习本身就是培养的。个人和社会的结果由他们自己负责。不召开教职员会议；不存在任何委员会。因此，有思想的人享有有利于反思和会议的条件。数学家可以心无旁骛地培养数学；其领域内的人文主义者、经济学家或政治学学生也可能如此。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S077")[#text("Administration has been minimized in extent and importance. Men without ideas, without power of concentration on ideas, would not be at home in the Institute.")]

#zh(id: "S077")[#text("行政管理的范围和重要性已被最小化。没有思想、没有集中思想的能力的人在研究所里不会感到自在。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S078")[#text("I can perhaps make this point clearer by citing briefly a few illustrations. A stipend was awarded to enable a Harvard professor to come to Princeton: he wrote asking,")]

#zh(id: "S078")[#text("我也许可以通过简要地引用一些例子来使这一点更加清楚。一位哈佛大学教授获得了一笔津贴来普林斯顿大学：他写信询问，")]

#html.p(id: "en-S079")[#text("\"What are my duties?\"")]

#zh(id: "S079")[#text("“我的职责是什么？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S080")[#text("I replied: \"You have no duties-only opportunities.\"")]

#zh(id: "S080")[#text("我回答说：“你没有只有职责的机会。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S081")[#text("An able young mathematician, having spent a year at Princeton, came to bid me good-by. As he was about to leave, he remarked:")]

#zh(id: "S081")[#text("一位能干的年轻数学家来向我告别，他在普林斯顿大学呆了一年。当他准备离开时，他说道：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S082")[#text("\"Perhaps you would like to know what this year has meant to me.\"")]

#zh(id: "S082")[#text("“也许你想知道今年对我来说意味着什么。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S083")[#text("\"Yes,\" I answered.")]

#zh(id: "S083")[#text("“是的，”我回答。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S084")[#text("\"Mathematics,\" he rejoined, \"is developing rapidly; the current literature is extensive. It is now over ten years since I took my Ph.D. degree. For a while I could keep up with my subject; but latterly that has become increasingly difficult and uncertain. Now, after a year here, the blinds are raised; the room is light; the windows are open. I have in my head two papers that I shall shortly write.\"")]

#zh(id: "S084")[#text("“数学，”他补充道，“发展迅速；当前的文献非常广泛。距离我获得博士学位已经十多年了。有一段时间我可以跟上我的学科，但后来变得越来越困难和不确定。现在，在这里一年后，百叶窗升起，房间明亮，窗户打开。我脑子里有两篇论文，我很快就会写。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S085")[#text("\"How long will this last?\" I asked.")]

#zh(id: "S085")[#text("“这会持续多久？”我问。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S086")[#text("\"Five years, perhaps ten.\"")]

#zh(id: "S086")[#text("“五年，也许十年。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S087")[#text("\"Then what?\"")]

#zh(id: "S087")[#text("“然后呢？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S088")[#text("\"I shall come back.\"")]

#zh(id: "S088")[#text("“我会回来的。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S089")[#text("A third example is of recent occurrence. A professor in a large Western university arrived in Princeton at the end of last December. He had in mind to resume some work with Professor Morey (at. Princeton University). But Morey suggested that he might find it worth while to see Panofsky and Swarzenski (at the Institute). Now he is busy with all three.")]

#zh(id: "S089")[#text("第三个例子是最近发生的。去年12月底，西方一所大型大学的一位教授抵达普林斯顿。他打算继续与莫雷教授（普林斯顿大学）一起工作。但莫雷表示，他可能会发现去见潘诺夫斯基和斯瓦岑斯基（在研究所）是值得的。现在他正忙着处理这三个人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S090")[#text("\"I shall stay,\" he added, \"until next October.\"")]

#zh(id: "S090")[#text("“我会留下来，”他补充道，“直到明年十月。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S091")[#text("\"You will find it hot in midsummer,\" I said.")]

#zh(id: "S091")[#text("“仲夏时节，你会发现天气很热。”我说。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S092")[#text("\"I shall be too busy and too happy to notice it.\"")]

#zh(id: "S092")[#text("“我会太忙太高兴而不会注意到它。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S093")[#text("Thus freedom brings not stagnation, but rather the danger of overwork. The wife of an English member recently asked:")]

#zh(id: "S093")[#text("因此，自由带来的不是停滞，而是过度劳累的危险。一位英国议员的妻子最近问道：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S094")[#text("\"Does everyone work until two o'clock in the morning?\"")]

#zh(id: "S094")[#text("“大家都工作到凌晨两点吗？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S095")[#text("The Institute has had thus far no building. At this moment the mathematicians are guests of the Princeton mathematicians in Fine Hall; some of the humanists are guests of the Princeton humanists in McCormick Hall; others work in rooms scattered through the town. The economists now occupy a suite at The Princeton Inn.")]

#zh(id: "S095")[#text("该研究所迄今为止还没有任何建筑物。此刻，数学家们是普林斯顿大学数学家们在法恩厅的客人；一些人文主义者是麦考密克大厅普林斯顿人文主义者的客人；其他人则在遍布城镇的房间里工作。经济学家们现在住在普林斯顿酒店的一间套房。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S096")[#text("My own quarters are located in an office building on Nassau Street, where I work among shopkeepers, dentists, lawyers, chiropractors, and groups of Princeton scholars conducting a local government survey and a study of population. Bricks and mortar are thus quite inessential, as President Gilman proved in Baltimore sixty-odd years ago. Nevertheless, we miss informal contact with one another and are about to remedy this defect by the erection of a building provided by the founders, to be called Fuld Hall.")]

#zh(id: "S096")[#text("我自己的宿舍位于拿骚街的一栋办公楼里，我在那里与店主、牙医、律师、脊椎按摩师以及进行地方政府调查和人口研究的普林斯顿学者团体一起工作。因此，正如吉尔曼总统六十多年前在巴尔的摩所证明的那样，砖头和灰浆是相当无关紧要的。然而，我们怀念彼此之间的非正式接触，并且即将通过建造由创始人提供的一栋建筑来弥补这一缺陷，这座建筑被称为富尔德大厅。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S097")[#text("But formality shall go no farther. The Institute must remain small; and it will hold fast to the conviction that The Institute Group desires leisure, security, freedom from organization and routine, and, finally, informal contacts with the scholars of Princeton University and others who from time to time can be lured to Princeton from distant places. Among these Niels Bohr has come from Copenhagen, von Laue from Berlin, Levi Civita from Rome, André Weil from Strasbourg, Dirac and G. H. Hardy from Cambridge, Pauli from Zurich, Lemaitre from Louvain, Wade-Gery from Oxford, and Americans from Harvard, Yale, Columbia, Cornell, Johns Hopkins, Chicago, California, and other centers of light and learning.")]

#zh(id: "S097")[#text("但形式已经到此为止了。该研究所必须保持规模较小；它将坚信，研究所集团渴望休闲、安全、不受组织和例行公事的自由，最后，与普林斯顿大学的学者和其他不时被从遥远的地方吸引到普林斯顿大学的学者进行非正式接触。其中尼尔斯·玻尔（Niels Bohr）来自哥本哈根，冯·劳厄（von Laue）来自柏林，列维·奇维塔（Levi Civita）来自罗马，安德烈·韦尔（André Weil）来自斯特拉斯堡，狄拉克（Dirac）和G. H. Hardy来自剑桥，泡利（Pauli）来自苏黎世，勒梅特（Lemaitre）来自鲁汶，韦德·格里（Wade-Gery）来自牛津，以及来自哈佛大学、耶鲁大学、哥伦比亚大学、康奈尔大学、约翰霍普金斯大学、芝加哥、加利福尼亚州和其他光与学习中心的美国人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S098")[#text("We make ourselves no promises, but we cherish the hope that the unobstructed pursuit of useless knowledge will prove to have consequences in the future as in the past. Not for a moment, however, do we defend the Institute on that ground. It exists as a paradise for scholars who, like poets and musicians, have won the right to do as they please and who accomplish most when enabled to do so.")]

#zh(id: "S098")[#text("我们不给自己做出任何承诺，但我们希望，对无用知识的畅通无阻的追求将像过去一样在未来产生后果。然而，我们暂时不会以此为理由为研究所辩护。对于像诗人和音乐家一样的学者来说，它是一个天堂，他们赢得了随心所欲的权利，并且在能够这样做时取得了最大的成就。")]
